{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_126583413#2_254044156","relevance":1,"reason":"The document states that premature children are among those at increased risk for abuse, but it does not explain why prematurity raises risk.","learned":"Prematurity is listed alongside first-born, unplanned, stepchildren, and handicapped status as a risk factor for child abuse in a medical education page."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature prematurity preterm) (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ stress burden difficult temperament crying))","reason":"Search for explanatory mechanisms such as parental stress, caregiving burden, difficult temperament, and crying associated with premature/preterm infants and maltreatment."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature prematurity preterm) (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ stress burden difficult temperament crying))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_421627504#3_457288719","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q739","rank":1,"snippet":"Ways to help See also References Further reading \" , \"segment\": \"Children who witness domestic violence also have a tendency to partake in high risk play activities, self-abuse, and suicide. Prenatal The physical effects of domestic violence on children, different than the effects of direct abuse, can start when they are a fetus in their mother's womb, which can result in low infant birth weights, premature birth, excessive bleeding, and fetal death due to the mother's physical trauma and emotional stress. Increased maternal <cover>stress during the times of abuse, especially when combined with smoking and drug abuse, can also lead to premature </cover>deliveries and low weight babies. When a woman is stressed while pregnant, the baby can be born with stress and anxiety and can sometimes have problems with growth. Infants Infant children who are present in the home where domestic violence occurs often fall victim to being \"caught in the crossfire.\" They may suffer physical injuries from unintentional trauma as their parent is suffering from abuse. Infants may be inconsolable and irritable, have a lack of responsiveness secondary to lacking the emotional and physical attachment to their mother, suffer from developmental delays, "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"1/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_421627504#3_457288719","relevance":1,"reason":"This discusses abuse/domestic violence during pregnancy leading to premature birth and low birth weight, which is the reverse direction of the information need and does not explain why prematurity increases later abuse risk.","learned":"Domestic violence and maternal stress can contribute to premature delivery, but this is not evidence that being premature is a child-abuse risk factor."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature prematurity preterm) (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ parental parent caregiver) (+ stress depression bonding))","reason":"Look for direct explanatory evidence that preterm birth increases maltreatment risk through parental stress, depression, caregiving burden, or impaired bonding."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature prematurity preterm) (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ parental parent caregiver) (+ stress depression bonding))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_394309708#10_812104109","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q740","rank":1,"snippet":"stress could trigger the body’s production of the human cortisol. Pregnancy seems to be very crucial. An expecting mother needs to very careful that she is not exposed to any infections, especially during the first trimmest. The second and third trimesters do not increase the risk of developing the disorder as much. Stress-inducing environmental factors that may be involved in schizophrenia include prenatal exposure to a viral infection, exposure to a virus during infancy, low oxygen levels during birth (from prolonged labor or <cover>premature birth), early parental loss or separation, and physical or sexual abuse in childhood (Understanding Schizophrenia). There is still not any evidence found to prove whether or not stress </cover>is a cause of schizophrenia. Emotionally significant events will precede an episode of illness, but the basis for the illness must already be present. Men who have children when they are 45-49 years of age and greater than 50 years of age had twice and three times the risk for developing schizophrenia, respectively, as the children of men less than 25 years of age. Maternal age has not been studied yet (Opler, 2008).\" , \"start_char\": 5841.000000 ,"}}
