{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_781862789#4_851364856","relevance":0,"reason":"The result is a broad infant-topic/navigation list that merely includes preterm birth, shaken baby syndrome, attachment, and child abuse as separate topics; it provides no explanatory evidence.","learned":"The hospitalization/bonding query can retrieve topic lists rather than evidence-bearing text."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ preterm premature prematurity) \"child maltreatment\" (+ stress parenting caregiver))","reason":"Use the formal phrase child maltreatment and require preterm/prematurity with parenting or caregiver stress to target research explaining the mechanism."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ preterm premature prematurity) \"child maltreatment\" (+ stress parenting caregiver))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1065794891#8_2188160896","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q742","rank":1,"snippet":"Summary References \" , \"segment\": \"11) Young children, especially those less than 1 year of age, have the highest rate of abuse and because of their smaller size they are particularly vulnerable to severe and fatal maltreatment. Any characteristic that that makes a child more difficult to care for predisposes them to maltreatment. Examples include children with chronic illnesses/special health care needs, emotional/behavioral disabilities, physical aggression/antisocial traits or physical/developmental disabilities. <cover>Premature delivery may also increase a child’s vulnerability to abuse. ( 2,16) Parents with a limited knowledge of normal child development, low sense of parenting competence, or harsh, inattentive, or inconsistent parenting may be predisposed to abuse their children. Rates of child maltreatment </cover>by unemployed parents are 2 to 3 times higher and substance abuse and mental illness are also more common. ( 11) Factors that decrease a parent’s ability to cope with stress increase the potential for maltreatment including: low self-esteem, poor impulse control, young age, low educational achievement, poor social support and mental illness. In addition, parents who were victims of child maltreatment themselves or who currently have an abusive partner are more likely to be abusive parents. ( "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"4/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1065794891#8_2188160896","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a child maltreatment risk-factor discussion that directly explains the mechanism: child characteristics that make care more difficult predispose to maltreatment, and premature delivery may increase vulnerability; it also connects risk to parental coping, competence, stress, and support.","learned":"Prematurity can raise maltreatment risk because premature infants may be more vulnerable or harder to care for, while parental low competence and poor coping with stress increase abuse potential."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q742","reason":"The current query found a strong grade-3 result; continue it to find more research or clinical sources using the same formal terminology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q742"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1470409485#0_3294255067","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q742","rank":2,"snippet":"prevention (ivp) branch Forbidden Violence and Social Determinants of Health Violence is a leading cause of injury, disability, and death. It impacts the health and well-being of all Californians – our families, neighbors, coworkers, schools, and communities. The consequences of violence are costly, and influence nearly all health and mental health outcomes throughout life. Violence contributes to these negative health outcomes through trauma and toxic stress.* Forms of Violence: ​<cover>Child Maltreatment ​Workplace violence ​Intimate partner violence ​Community violence and trauma ​Teen dating violence ​Gang violence ​Sexual violence ​Gun violence ​Bullying/harassment ​Police-involved violence ​Youth violence ​Crime (assault, robbery) ​Elder maltreatment ​Hate crimes ​Suicide ​Terrorism Early childhood exposure to violence (child abuse, intimate partner violence) and other chronic stress (poverty, neglect, and emotional abuse) can result in injury, disease, and premature </cover>death. A growing body of research on adverse childhood experiences has demonstrated that toxic, chronic stress harms brain development and leads to lifelong effects on learning, behavior, and health. ​Violence itself is a social determinant of health; violence may also be a result of the environments where people live and children grow. For example, those who grow up and live in environments with limited social, educational, and economic opportunities "}}
