{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_640062589#0_1116001376","relevance":1,"reason":"This nursing diagnosis page lists premature or ill infants as risk factors for impaired parent-infant attachment because they may be unable to initiate parental contact and because of separation/barriers. It supports a possible mechanism but does not discuss child abuse or maltreatment risk directly.","learned":"Prematurity may impair parent-infant attachment through infant illness, altered behavioral organization, and separation/physical barriers, which could be relevant to abuse risk but is not direct evidence here."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"premature delivery may also increase\" \"vulnerability to abuse\")","reason":"Search an exact phrase from the strongest prior grade-3 result to find its source or related documents that directly state and explain prematurity increasing vulnerability to abuse."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"premature delivery may also increase\" \"vulnerability to abuse\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1065794891#7_2188158776","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q748","rank":1,"snippet":"and classes but it is well known that children in socioeconomically disadvantaged families are at greater risk. Compared with white, Native American, and Hispanic children, there is a significantly higher rate of maltreatment of African-American children with a higher mortality rate. ( 11) Young children, especially those less than 1 year of age, have the highest rate of abuse and because of their smaller size they are particularly vulnerable to severe and fatal maltreatment. Any characteristic that that makes a child more difficult to care for predisposes them to maltreatment. Examples include children with chronic illnesses/special health care needs, emotional/behavioral disabilities, physical aggression/antisocial traits or physical/developmental disabilities. <cover>Premature delivery may also increase a child’s vulnerability to abuse. ( </cover>2,16) Parents with a limited knowledge of normal child development, low sense of parenting competence, or harsh, inattentive, or inconsistent parenting may be predisposed to abuse their children.\" , \"start_char\": 4934.000000 , \"title\": \"Child Abuse (Non-accidental Trauma)\" , \"url\": \"https://www.saem.org/cdem/education/online-education/peds-em-curriculum/gu-ob/child-abuse-(non-accidental-trauma)\" }"}}
