{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1278267280#6_2315521261","relevance":1,"reason":"This general child-abuse page lists caregiver risk factors such as family stress, isolation, poor parenting skills, and having a developmentally or physically disabled child in the family. It supports a general special-needs/stress pathway but does not mention prematurity or explain why premature birth itself raises abuse risk.","learned":"General child-abuse risk-factor summaries identify child disability/special needs and caregiver stress, isolation, and poor parenting skills as abuse risks, but this result lacks a prematurity connection."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"premature infant\" \"premature infants\" \"preterm infant\" \"preterm infants\") (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ \"parenting stress\" \"caregiver burden\" \"parental stress\" \"difficult to care\"))","reason":"Search directly for preterm/premature infant maltreatment evidence with explicit parenting stress, caregiver burden, or difficult-care mechanisms."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"premature infant\" \"premature infants\" \"preterm infant\" \"preterm infants\") (+ maltreatment abuse neglect) (+ \"parenting stress\" \"caregiver burden\" \"parental stress\" \"difficult to care\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_261468532#13_384608534","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q770","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_261468532#13_384608534\" , \"end_char\": 6267.000000 , \"headings\": \"Parenting Stress Index, Short Form<cover>Parenting Stress Index, Short Form Overview Acronym: Authors: Citation: Domain Assessed: Age Range: Measure Type: Measure Format: Administration Training Parallel or Alternate Forms Psychometrics Translations Population Information Pros & Cons/References \" , \"segment\": \"Maternal scores on the PSI-SF have also been found to be related to increased risk for developing insecure attachment in a sample of premature infants (Laganiére, Tessier, & Nadeau, 2003). In addition, maternal parenting concerns during pregnancy are related to later PSI-SF scores (Combs-Orme, Cain, & Wilson, 2004). The PSI/SF has been found to correlate with the Full-Length form: Total Stress and Total Stress=.94, Parental Distress and Parent Domain=.92, Difficult Child and Child Domain=.87. PSI-SF scores decreased significantly following a prevention program for neglect </cover>conducted with a high-risk, predominantly African American sample. Improvements were maintained at 6-month follow-up (DePanfilis & Dubowitz, 2005). They have also been found to change following participation in a child crisis care program (rural ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_261468532#12_384606531","rank":1,"relevance":1}]}}
