{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_680964661#26_1341243902","relevance":2,"reason":"This University of Iowa non-accidental trauma case/resource is dedicated to abuse-related trauma and directly lists infant characteristics that make abuse more likely, including frequent crying, premature or low birth weight, and special needs/disabilities. It is useful supporting evidence, but it does not explicitly explain why prematurity increases abuse risk beyond grouping it with vulnerable/high-need infant traits.","learned":"Medical non-accidental trauma sources identify premature or low-birth-weight infants as more likely to be abused, alongside age under 6 months, frequent crying, and special needs/disabilities."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"premature infant\" \"premature babies\" \"low birth weight\") (+ \"more likely to be abused\" \"risk of child abuse\" \"risk for child abuse\") (+ crying \"special needs\" stress))","reason":"Search for direct wording that premature or low-birth-weight infants are more likely to be abused, with crying/special-needs/stress terms likely to reveal the explanation."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"premature infant\" \"premature babies\" \"low birth weight\") (+ \"more likely to be abused\" \"risk of child abuse\" \"risk for child abuse\") (+ crying \"special needs\" stress))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_216693227#9_239469646","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q774","rank":1,"snippet":"been associated with miscarriage, late prenatal care, stillbirth, preterm birth, fetal injury (including bruising, broken and fractured bones, stab wounds and <cover>low birth weight. Violence during pregnancy also leads to additional risks for the mother such as increased mental health problems, suicide attempts, worsening of chronic illness, injury, substance abuse, anxiety, stress, chronic pain, and gynecological problems. Women battered during pregnancy were more frequently and severely beaten throughout the course of their relationship compared to women who were not abused during pregnancy. IPV also accounts for a large portion of maternal mortality. Homicide is the second leading cause of injury related deaths in pregnant and post-partum women in the United States and a study conducted in hospital in India found that 16% of all deaths during pregnancy were a result of partner violence. Studies have also found a correlation between domestic violence and increased use of abortion. Pregnant abused women are less likely to report abuse or leave their abuser because of added financial and housing security concerns. Risk factors Certain women are more likely to be abused </cover>during pregnancy than others.\" , \"start_char\": 7687.000000 , \"title\": \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_216693227#9_239469646","relevance":1,"reason":"This result is about intimate partner violence during pregnancy causing preterm birth and low birth weight, and about risk factors for pregnant women being abused. It is the reverse direction and does not explain why premature infants are at increased risk of child abuse.","learned":"Domestic violence during pregnancy can contribute to preterm birth and low birth weight, but this does not answer why prematurity increases later child-abuse risk."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"preterm birth\" prematurity premature \"low birth weight\") (+ \"child protective services\" \"child maltreatment\" \"child abuse\") (+ study risk odds))","reason":"Search for research evidence directly linking preterm birth or low birth weight with later child maltreatment or child protective services involvement."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"preterm birth\" prematurity premature \"low birth weight\") (+ \"child protective services\" \"child maltreatment\" \"child abuse\") (+ study risk odds))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q775","rank":2,"snippet":"with child abuse include: lack of knowledge about child development lack of knowledge about how to care for children insufficient money/income to support the needs of a child substance abuse psychological problems unbalanced family relationships domestic violence single parenting. The risk of child abuse is increased if a child's parents are young and immature, with unrealistic expectations. Parents who are young and immature may lack knowledge about child development and how to take care of children, as well as the cost of raising a child, leading to unrealistic expectations. These parents may become frustrated when their expectations aren't met. Families with children who are <cover>premature, chronically ill, or who have attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder or a mental health disorder are also at a higher risk for child abuse. </cover>These children will have more care requirements, potentially leading to burnout and increasing the risk of abuse.\" , \"start_char\": 4712.000000 , \"title\": \"The red flags of child abuse : Nursing made Incredibly Easy\" , \"url\": \"https://journals.lww.com/nursingmadeincrediblyeasy/Fulltext/2018/03000/The_red_flags_of_child_abuse.8.aspx\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_126583413#2_254044156","rank":1,"relevance":1}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"38/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","relevance":3,"reason":"This nursing article is dedicated to child-abuse red flags/risk factors and directly explains why prematurity is a risk factor: premature or chronically ill children have more care requirements, which can lead to caregiver burnout and increased abuse risk.","learned":"Premature children may be at higher risk for child abuse because their increased care requirements can overwhelm caregivers, cause burnout, and contribute to frustration or abuse."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q775","reason":"This query retrieved a strong direct explanatory result; continue it to find more research or clinical sources linking prematurity/low birth weight with child maltreatment or child protective services risk."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q775"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#10_3378522351","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q775","rank":3,"snippet":"Substance abuse consider this Figure cheat sheet Red flags Figure on the web Figure \" , \"segment\": \"The risk of child abuse is increased if a child's parents are young and immature, with unrealistic expectations. Parents who are young and immature may lack knowledge about child development and how to take care of children, as well as the cost of raising a child, leading to unrealistic expectations. These parents may become frustrated when their expectations aren't met. Families with children who are <cover>premature, chronically ill, or who have attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder or a mental health disorder are also at a higher risk for child abuse. </cover>These children will have more care requirements, potentially leading to burnout and increasing the risk of abuse. Lastly, families in which there's a nonbiological father or maternal boyfriend have an increased risk of sexual abuse. Protective factors that can decrease the risk of child abuse include: supportive family environments supportive social networks parental employment adequate housing access to healthcare. Signs and symptoms A variety of signs and symptoms are associated with child abuse, including physical signs and emotional symptoms, and certain family dynamics. Children who are abused "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"39/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#10_3378522351","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another directly relevant segment from the Nursing Made Incredibly Easy article. It states that families with premature children are at higher risk for child abuse because these children have more care requirements, which can lead to caregiver burnout and increased abuse risk.","learned":"Prematurity raises abuse risk by increasing caregiving demands; greater care requirements can produce caregiver burnout and frustration, contributing to abuse."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature chronically) (+ \"more care requirements\" burnout) (+ \"child abuse\" \"risk of abuse\"))","reason":"Search exact explanatory wording from the strong nursing article to find additional sources or segments that directly state the care-requirements/burnout mechanism for premature children and abuse risk."}}
