{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_38_686175262#4_1477230664","relevance":0,"reason":"This result is about septic drainfield failure; 'premature' and 'neglect' are used in a home-maintenance context and have nothing to do with premature birth or child abuse.","learned":"The burnout/overwhelmed/frustration query can drift badly when 'premature' and 'neglect' are not constrained to infants or children."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ prematurity premature preterm \"low birth weight\") (+ \"medical child abuse\" \"child abuse\" \"child maltreatment\") (+ \"risk factors\" \"increased risk\") (+ disability \"special health care needs\" illness))","reason":"Search for medical/clinical child-abuse risk-factor sources that group prematurity or low birth weight with disability, illness, or special health care needs, which may provide direct explanatory evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ prematurity premature preterm \"low birth weight\") (+ \"medical child abuse\" \"child abuse\" \"child maltreatment\") (+ \"risk factors\" \"increased risk\") (+ disability \"special health care needs\" illness))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_412722221#10_1043994063","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q782","rank":2,"snippet":"and their families—significantly boosting the promise of a bright future for children, families, and communities. 3. Include at-risk infants and toddlers in the state’s definition of eligibility for Part C services. Part C allows states to provide services to infants and toddlers who are at risk of developmental delay. States may include children with a history of significant biological or medical conditions (e.g., <cover>low birth weight or failure to thrive) and/or children who are at risk due to environmental factors such as poverty, homelessness, substantiated child abuse or neglect, parental age, parental illegal substance abuse, and parental mental illness. As of February 2008, only five out of 56 states and territories serve at-risk children, even though it is well documented that young children with multiple risk factors </cover>are the most vulnerable to poor health and development. States should expand their definitions to include these at-risk infants. 4. Fully implement the Part C and the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) mandates requiring states to refer to Part C all children under the age of 3 with substantiated cases of abuse, neglect, and drug exposure. Both the IDEA 2004 and CAPTA 2003 reauthorizations ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1065794891#8_2188160896","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
