{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_412722221#10_1043994063","relevance":1,"reason":"This policy/resource passage discusses low birth weight and child abuse/neglect as separate eligibility or developmental-risk factors for infants and toddlers. It does not explain why being born premature or low birth weight increases the risk of being abused.","learned":"Low birth weight is sometimes treated as a biological/medical risk factor in early-childhood services, while abuse/neglect is an environmental risk factor, but this does not directly answer the prematurity-to-abuse question."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"children who are premature\" \"born prematurely\" \"premature children\" \"premature infant\") (+ \"higher risk\" \"increased risk\" \"risk of child abuse\") (+ \"care requirements\" burnout overwhelmed stress))","reason":"Search for direct explanatory wording like the strong nursing article: premature children have higher child-abuse risk because increased care requirements can cause caregiver stress, overwhelm, or burnout."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"children who are premature\" \"born prematurely\" \"premature children\" \"premature infant\") (+ \"higher risk\" \"increased risk\" \"risk of child abuse\") (+ \"care requirements\" burnout overwhelmed stress))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_768237216#11_1614276714","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q783","rank":3,"snippet":"will my family’s life be changed? Having a baby hospitalized after birth is a stressful experience for families. However, despite the long-awaited arrival in the home, stress can increase for  caregivers after discharge from the NICU. Families sometimes feel very attached to,  or dependent on, the NICU care team. They may feel alone or fear that they are  unable to give proper care for the baby at home. This stress can put mothers at  <cover>increased risk of postpartum depression and sometimes the stress can affect how  families and marriages function. Assessing for family functioning, postpartum  depression, and confidence in taking care of the baby at home are important  components of the medical home. If available, the medical home care coordinator or  social worker and the local Early Intervention Part C Program may provide additional support to  the family. Finding support within the special needs community can be helpful for  families.\" , \"start_char\": 5068.000000 , \"title\": \"Medical Home Portal - Premature Infant </cover>Follow-Up\" , \"url\": \"https://www.medicalhomeportal.org/living-with-child/diagnoses-and-conditions---faqs/premature-infant-follow-up\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#10_3378522351","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_768237216#11_1614276714","relevance":1,"reason":"This premature-infant follow-up page explains that NICU hospitalization and post-discharge care can increase caregiver stress, depression risk, and lack of confidence caring for the baby. This supports a plausible stress mechanism, but it does not mention child abuse or maltreatment.","learned":"Premature infants can create substantial post-NICU caregiver stress, fear, and reduced confidence in home care; family support and screening for depression/functioning are recommended."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"shaken baby syndrome\" (+ premature prematurity \"low birth weight\") (+ \"risk factors\" \"risk factor\") (+ crying frustration caregiver))","reason":"Search for additional shaken baby syndrome/abusive head trauma sources that directly list prematurity or low birth weight as risk factors and may explain the caregiver crying/frustration mechanism."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"shaken baby syndrome\" (+ premature prematurity \"low birth weight\") (+ \"risk factors\" \"risk factor\") (+ crying frustration caregiver))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_930063563#13_2018967566","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q784","rank":2,"snippet":"shaken baby syndrome (as well as other types of inflicted trauma on children) may be conceptualized into two broad categories: Family risk factors Young and/or single parents Lower educational background Unstable family dynamics Financial/food/housing concerns and stresses Domestic violence Drug/ alcohol abuse Parental mental illness, especially postpartum depression Limited or no immediate support system (for example, relatives, church groups, etc.) Unrelated adults staying in the home Infant risk factors Perinatal <cover>risk factors (for example, threatened miscarriage, prematurity, infant malformations, multiple births (twins, triplets, etc.) Colicky infants -- often aggravated by the natural history of increase in infant crying during the first two months of life (see above) Male gender Unwanted pregnancy SLIDESHOW Concussions & Brain Injuries: Symptoms, Tests, Treatment See Slideshow What are shaken baby syndrome </cover>symptoms and signs? Readers Comments 2 Share Your Story The symptoms and signs of shaken baby syndrome have an extremely broad range to display. This range is due to the nature of the inflicted trauma, including the frequency, duration, and whether the result of a single event or multiple events. Behavioral changes may include the following: Extreme irritability and high-pitched crying Lethargy and poor feeding Vomiting without obvious reason Loss of social ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_930063563#12_2018964925","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
